---
title: Element 4: If "No" was Responded to any of the First Three Questions, Provide Explanation
---
sort_key: 4
---
menu: /help/guides/nih
---
body:

#### Question

If you answered "No" to elements 1, 2, or 3, or if you anticipate that sharing will be limited in some other way, please describe these limitations and the ethical, legal, or technical factors for them. Your response should specify a particular reason(s) for limiting sharing.

#### Guidance for Response

To address the time-based questions (Elements 2 and 3) first, use your response to this Element to explain any reasons why data sharing may need to take place later than the recommended deadlines (e.g., student involvement in a project, patents/licenses, data use agreements, etc.)

Also use this space to explain why data may not be made available for as long as any required or expected retention periods, as outlined by a journal, etc.

If a limitation to the maximum amount of data sharing must be explained (e.g., a "No" response to Element 1), consult NIH's FAQ on [What are justifiable reasons for limiting sharing of data](https://grants.nih.gov/faqs#/data-management-and-sharing-policy.htm?anchor=56549).

Justifiable reasons to limit data sharing include:

- The project's informed consents limit the scope of data sharing
- Existing consent (e.g., for previously collected biospecimens) prohibits sharing or limits the scope or extent of sharing
- A dataset is impossible to de-identify sufficiently to guarantee that the privacy and safety of the research participants can be protected (and [Certificates of Confidentiality](https://grants.nih.gov/policy-and-compliance/policy-topics/human-subjects/coc) would also be insufficient)
- Datasets cannot be digitized with reasonable efforts.

!!! info
    **Zenodo note:** While sensitive datasets should never be shared through Zenodo, Restricted or Closed Access uploads are possible for datasets that cannot be deposited as Open Access. Open Access data deposits and metadata records are fully visible and available to the public. Restricted/Closed Access uploads are not visible on the front page, but are still discoverable through any communities in which they are included, via the DOI, and through search queries. Data depositors control whether their deposits and records are Open Access, Restricted/Closed Access, or Embargoed.
    <br><br>
    Licenses in Zenodo empower depositors to outline the ways in which downloads of their materials may be used. Users are responsible for monitoring subsequent uses of their materials. All metadata (e.g., the descriptive metadata comprising the non-file portions of a Zenodo record) is licensed CC0 (public domain), and can be exported and harvested via the OAI-PMH protocol. See the Terms of Use and Policies for more details on users' rights and responsibilities with regard to access and re-use of their data.

